WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:04.500
Do you enjoy sipping drinks through plastic straws?

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.668
Well if activists get their way,

00:00:06.668 --> 00:00:08.804
you won't be allowed to anymore.

00:00:08.804 --> 00:00:11.340
These must be banned, they say.

00:00:11.340 --> 00:00:12.280
Why?

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:14.520
500 million plastic straws

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:15.920
are used in this country

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:18.020
every single day.

00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:24.160
When celebrities speak, politicians listen.

00:00:24.160 --> 00:00:26.420
Seattle recently banned straws

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:28.440
and other places want to do the same.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:29.480
Straws suck

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:32.120
and we are no longer going to allow for plastic straws

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:33.760
here in San Francisco.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:35.248
Their time has come and gone.

00:00:35.248 --> 00:00:37.160
The idea that you're gonna ban straws

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.280
and save the world is ridiculous.

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:42.180
Angela Logomasini studies environmental issues

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:44.300
at the Competitive Enterprise Institute.

00:00:44.360 --> 00:00:47.140
What will banning plastic straws accomplish?

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:48.680
Probably nothing at all.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.120
It might make some people in Hollywood feel good,

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.000
it might make some politicians feel good,

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:54.500
like they're doing something,

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:55.980
it might sound good at parties,

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:58.060
but it's not gonna solve any problems.

00:00:58.060 --> 00:01:01.800
Plastic pollution in the ocean is a real problem,

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:04.120
but only about 1% of it

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.200
comes from the United States.

00:01:06.200 --> 00:01:11.240
Of that 1%, a tiny amount comes from plastic straws.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:12.560
But what about that

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:14.660
500 million number?

00:01:14.660 --> 00:01:16.240
In the USA alone,

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.620
over 500 million straws are being used every single day,

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:22.200
most of which are going into our oceans.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:23.420
Americans toss out

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:26.060
500 million straws each day.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.300
An incredible 500 million plastic straws.

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:30.260
Where do they all get that number?

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:31.974
Every piece of plastic.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:35.100
They got it from this child's school project.

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:38.260
He says he got the 500 million straws a day figure

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:40.180
through a phone survey he conducted

00:01:40.180 --> 00:01:42.300
with straw manufacturers.

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:45.620
I started the project to reduce the number of straws.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:49.060
The media just accepts his 500 million number.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:51.580
But the actual number is much lower

00:01:51.580 --> 00:01:53.920
according to the analysis firm Technomic.

00:01:53.920 --> 00:01:57.840
Okay, so activists and media exaggerate the problem.

00:01:57.840 --> 00:01:58.900
500 million.

00:01:58.900 --> 00:02:00.020
500 million.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:01.660
500 million.

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:02.840
Still they promised

00:02:02.840 --> 00:02:05.460
it would be easy to get rid of the straws.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:07.020
If we can reduce

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:08.180
something that is easy,

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:09.880
that is polluting to the environment,

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:11.640
that gets stuck in turtles' noses

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:13.470
and causes damage to the environment,

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:14.356
let's do that.

00:02:14.360 --> 00:02:17.160
Progressive talk show host Ethan Bearman.

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:20.780
Sometimes we do need a little gentle, guiding hand

00:02:20.780 --> 00:02:21.720
from government.

00:02:21.720 --> 00:02:23.940
Government's gentle, guiding hand

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:25.680
will either ban straws

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:29.020
or order us to replace this cheap plastic straw

00:02:29.020 --> 00:02:30.100
with ones like these

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:31.840
made of paper or bamboo.

00:02:31.840 --> 00:02:33.840
Plastic doesn't actually biodegrade,

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:35.140
so unlike a lot of other things

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:36.100
paper, for example

00:02:36.100 --> 00:02:38.140
actually breaks down into other components.

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:39.960
That's not a good thing.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:42.100
That means paper straws break down

00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:43.827
even while you're using them.

00:02:43.827 --> 00:02:45.060
They get soggy,

00:02:45.060 --> 00:02:46.520
they leak.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:47.680
That's the beauty of plastic.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:48.980
It's enduring.

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:50.360
A paper straw is gonna break down

00:02:50.360 --> 00:02:51.380
while you're drinking with it.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:54.820
Paper and bamboo straws aren't

00:02:54.820 --> 00:02:56.680
environmentally pristine either.

00:02:56.687 --> 00:02:58.520
Paper products take more energy

00:02:58.520 --> 00:02:59.980
and more effort to produce.

00:02:59.980 --> 00:03:02.100
You're gonna have a net negative environmental impact.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:03.880
The paper doesn't degrade in a landfill either.

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:06.220
Everything is essentially mummified.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:09.520
You're replacing a superior product with an inferior one

00:03:09.520 --> 00:03:11.720
and you're asking people to pay more for it.

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:13.040
Doesn't make a lot of sense.

00:03:13.040 --> 00:03:16.480
It doesn't, and the higher price is the final result.

00:03:16.480 --> 00:03:19.100
Paper straws are eight times more expensive

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:21.280
to make than plastic straws.

00:03:21.280 --> 00:03:23.000
Won't this hurt businesses?

00:03:23.000 --> 00:03:24.180
I don't think so.

00:03:24.180 --> 00:03:25.600
Maybe people won't use straws.

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:27.720
If it's $1.79 to get the fountain drink

00:03:27.720 --> 00:03:29.600
at Joe's corner deli

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:30.900
that we're talking about,

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.580
and now it's $1.83,

00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:35.420
I don't see that as being a huge difference

00:03:35.420 --> 00:03:36.220
in the price.

00:03:36.220 --> 00:03:37.520
This is what environmentalists say about

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:39.100
every policy they put out.

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:39.902
A few cents here,

00:03:39.902 --> 00:03:40.751
a few cents there,

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:42.500
eventually it begins to be a burden.

00:03:42.500 --> 00:03:43.615
We don't need straws.

00:03:43.615 --> 00:03:46.352
You know a lot of things in life are not necessary

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:48.360
but beneficial and enjoyable.

00:03:48.360 --> 00:03:50.020
Banning straws isn't gonna do anything

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:51.060
for the environment.

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:52.420
So what they're just trying to do

00:03:52.420 --> 00:03:55.020
is take away my freedom for nothing in return.

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:57.140
As the environment has become cleaner,

00:03:57.140 --> 00:03:58.580
that's become a specialty

00:03:58.580 --> 00:04:00.420
of the environmental movement -

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:01.980
spend your money

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:03.260
on feel-good policies

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:05.668
that make no real difference.

